1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,610
On 18 October 1965, a helicopter
disappears over the jungle of Vietnam.

2
00:00:06,640 --> 00:00:11,870
On board is a captain of the American
Special Forces: Larry Thorne.

3
00:00:11,890 --> 00:00:15,790
Only 34 years later is
the crash site found.

4
00:00:15,810 --> 00:00:19,180
Yet this man wore three
different uniforms in his life.

5
00:00:19,180 --> 00:00:24,480
He fought for Finland against the Soviet
Union, he served in the Waffen-SS,

6
00:00:24,480 --> 00:00:27,990
and he died as an officer of the US Army.

7
00:00:28,010 --> 00:00:33,390
In his homeland he was known
by another name: Lauri Törni.

8
00:00:33,420 --> 00:00:40,190
Lauri Allan Törni was born on 28
May 1919 in Viipuri, a port city

9
00:00:40,190 --> 00:00:42,050
in Karelia.

10
00:00:42,050 --> 00:00:47,720
At the time it belonged to Finland;
today it is called Vyborg and lies in Russia.

11
00:00:47,740 --> 00:00:51,310
In 1938 he was called
up for military service.

12
00:00:51,310 --> 00:00:58,000
A year later, in November 1939,
the Soviet Union attacked Finland.

13
00:00:58,030 --> 00:01:01,970
In the Winter War,
Törni fought north of Lake Ladoga,

14
00:01:01,970 --> 00:01:07,010
where Finnish units destroyed
encircled Soviet troops near Lemetti.

15
00:01:07,030 --> 00:01:12,790
When the war ended in March 1940,
he was a reserve lieutenant.

16
00:01:12,810 --> 00:01:15,710
Finland had to give up
large parts of Karelia,

17
00:01:15,710 --> 00:01:18,950
including his hometown of Viipuri.

18
00:01:18,970 --> 00:01:21,880
Finland now sought support from Germany.

19
00:01:21,880 --> 00:01:28,820
In June 1941, Törni travelled to Vienna and
completed several weeks of training with the

20
00:01:28,820 --> 00:01:30,460
Waffen-SS there.

21
00:01:30,480 --> 00:01:33,630
That same summer he returned
to Finland, because the

22
00:01:33,630 --> 00:01:36,130
Continuation War was beginning there.

23
00:01:36,150 --> 00:01:41,640
In 1943 he was given command of
a small unit that was soon simply

24
00:01:41,640 --> 00:01:44,000
called Detachment Törni.

25
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,940
His men operated far behind Soviet lines,

26
00:01:47,940 --> 00:01:51,860
laid ambushes and often
returned only after days.

27
00:01:51,890 --> 00:01:56,950
According to reports, the Soviet side
put a bounty of three million Finnish

28
00:01:56,950 --> 00:01:58,490
marks on him.

29
00:01:58,510 --> 00:02:03,160
In the summer of 1944 he fought
at Ilomantsi, in one of the

30
00:02:03,160 --> 00:02:06,040
last great battles of this war.

31
00:02:06,060 --> 00:02:10,310
One of the soldiers under his
command was Mauno Koivisto,

32
00:02:10,310 --> 00:02:13,590
who decades later became
President of Finland.

33
00:02:13,610 --> 00:02:18,470
In July 1944, Törni received
the Mannerheim Cross,

34
00:02:18,470 --> 00:02:21,540
Finland's highest military decoration.

35
00:02:21,580 --> 00:02:27,440
In September 1944, Finland concluded
an armistice with the Soviet Union.

36
00:02:27,440 --> 00:02:30,420
For Törni, the war was not over.

37
00:02:30,440 --> 00:02:32,160
He joined a group

38
00:02:32,160 --> 00:02:35,500
that wanted to resist a Soviet occupation,

39
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:42,420
and in January 1945 he went to
Germany to be trained as a saboteur.

40
00:02:42,450 --> 00:02:46,860
In the last weeks of the
war he fought in a Waffen-SS

41
00:02:46,860 --> 00:02:52,930
unit in northern Germany and surrendered
to British troops in May 1945.

42
00:02:52,950 --> 00:02:58,200
He escaped from the prison camp near
Lübeck and made his way back to Finland.

43
00:02:58,220 --> 00:03:00,680
There the state police arrested him.

44
00:03:00,710 --> 00:03:03,220
These images come from his file.

45
00:03:03,240 --> 00:03:06,940
He was sentenced to six
years in prison for treason.

46
00:03:06,940 --> 00:03:11,930
Twice he escaped from prison,
twice he was caught again.

47
00:03:11,960 --> 00:03:17,820
In December 1948, President
Juho Paasikivi pardoned him.

48
00:03:17,840 --> 00:03:21,020
But Törni no longer saw
a future in Finland.

49
00:03:21,040 --> 00:03:26,260
In 1949 he went to Sweden and signed
on under a false name aboard a ship

50
00:03:26,260 --> 00:03:28,190
bound for Venezuela.

51
00:03:28,210 --> 00:03:32,780
From there he sailed as a seaman
on a freighter heading for the USA.

52
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:39,250
In the Gulf of Mexico, off the coast of Alabama,
he jumped overboard and swam ashore.

53
00:03:39,270 --> 00:03:45,260
A new law opened a path for him:
the Lodge Act allowed foreigners to

54
00:03:45,260 --> 00:03:49,980
serve in the US Army and
thereby gain citizenship.

55
00:03:50,010 --> 00:03:57,810
In 1954, Lauri Törni joined the American
army under the name Larry Thorne.

56
00:03:57,830 --> 00:04:01,510
He joined the Special Forces,
the Green Berets,

57
00:04:01,510 --> 00:04:04,710
became a citizen and
eventually an officer.

58
00:04:04,740 --> 00:04:10,970
In 1962, operating from Bad Tölz,
he led a recovery mission in Iran.

59
00:04:10,990 --> 00:04:17,410
At an altitude of around 3,800 metres in the
Zagros Mountains, his team recovered the dead

60
00:04:17,410 --> 00:04:21,760
of a crashed US aircraft
and classified material,

61
00:04:21,760 --> 00:04:24,480
after earlier search parties had failed.

62
00:04:24,530 --> 00:04:29,040
From 1963, Thorne was deployed in Vietnam.

63
00:04:29,060 --> 00:04:33,540
In 1965 he belonged to a secret
unit tasked with bringing

64
00:04:33,540 --> 00:04:37,610
reconnaissance teams across
the border into Laos.

65
00:04:37,640 --> 00:04:44,260
On 18 October 1965 he took off from
Kham Duc in a helicopter of the South

66
00:04:44,260 --> 00:04:46,540
Vietnamese Air Force.

67
00:04:46,570 --> 00:04:50,950
In bad weather the aircraft
crashed into a forested mountain

68
00:04:50,950 --> 00:04:53,050
area southwest of Da Nang.

69
00:04:53,070 --> 00:04:55,310
The search was unsuccessful.

70
00:04:55,330 --> 00:04:59,890
Thorne was listed as missing
and was later promoted to major.

71
00:04:59,910 --> 00:05:06,240
Only in 1999 did an American-Vietnamese
team find the crash site

72
00:05:06,240 --> 00:05:08,460
and his remains.

73
00:05:08,480 --> 00:05:15,360
On 26 June 2003 he was buried
at Arlington National Cemetery,

74
00:05:15,360 --> 00:05:17,970
under the name Larry Thorne.

75
00:05:17,990 --> 00:05:23,150
Three uniforms, two names and
a life between the fronts.

76
00:05:23,170 --> 00:05:27,970
In Finland, many still regard
Lauri Törni as a war hero,

77
00:05:28,150 --> 00:05:31,850
while others point to his
service in the Waffen-SS.

78
00:05:31,880 --> 00:05:33,260
His story shows how

79
00:05:33,260 --> 00:05:38,590
closely the fates of individual people
in the 20th century were bound up

80
00:05:38,590 --> 00:05:40,990
with the great conflicts.

81
00:05:41,020 --> 00:05:43,260
How do you see Lauri Törni?

82
00:05:43,280 --> 00:05:45,200
Write it in the comments.

83
00:05:45,220 --> 00:05:47,460
If you enjoyed this story,

84
00:05:47,460 --> 00:05:52,540
subscribe to Chronik der Macht and
check out the next documentary.

